{"type":"result","result":"```json\n{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Path traversal validation missing resolve() in isolation.py _check_request_path\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py\",\n      \"line\": 195,\n      \"line_range_end\": 210,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The _check_request_path method validates paths using `requested.is_relative_to(self.scratch_root)` without first calling `requested.resolve()`. If `requested` contains symlinks, the is_relative_to check operates on the unresolved path, allowing an attacker to pass validation with a path like `scratch_root/link_to_outside/file` where link_to_outside points to /etc.\",\n      \"why\": \"Symlinks can bypass containment checks that operate on unresolved paths. An attacker who can create symlinks in scratch_root can construct paths that pass is_relative_to but resolve to arbitrary locations.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Call requested.resolve() before the is_relative_to check: `requested = requested.resolve(); if not requested.is_relative_to(self.scratch_root.resolve()): raise PermissionError(...)`\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"isolation.py:195-210\", \"isolation.py:247-262 (_check_allowed_write)\", \"isolation.py:289-304 (_check_allowed_read)\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"TOCTOU race between path validation and file operation in isolation.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py\",\n      \"line\": 195,\n      \"line_range_end\": 210,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"All validation methods (_check_request_path, _check_allowed_write, _check_allowed_read) validate the path and return, but the actual file operation happens later. Between validation and operation, an attacker with filesystem access could replace a validated path with a symlink pointing outside scratch_root.\",\n      \"why\": \"Time-of-check to time-of-use gap allows filesystem substitution attacks. If the evaluation agent itself can modify the filesystem (which is the point of scratch_root), it can race validation.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use O_NOFOLLOW flag when opening files, or validate after opening using os.fstat() to check the opened file is still inside scratch_root. Consider using fd-based operations exclusively after initial open.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"isolation.py:195-304\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Symlink following not restricted in disk_budget.py walk_directory\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/disk_budget.py\",\n      \"line\": 156,\n      \"line_range_end\": 170,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The walk_directory method uses `for entry in root_path.rglob('*'):` without `followlinks=False` equivalent. Path.rglob follows symlinks by default. An attacker could create a symlink to a large external directory (e.g., /usr) to make quota calculations count external bytes, or create symlink loops to cause DoS.\",\n      \"why\": \"Following symlinks during quota calculation can count bytes outside scratch_root, giving incorrect quota usage. Symlink loops can cause infinite traversal. Both violate isolation assumptions.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Check `entry.is_symlink()` and skip symlinks: `for entry in root_path.rglob('*'): if entry.is_symlink(): continue; ...`\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"disk_budget.py:156-170\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hardlink quota bypass in disk_budget.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/disk_budget.py\",\n      \"line\": 156,\n      \"line_range_end\": 170,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The walk_directory method sums file sizes with `total_bytes += entry.stat().st_size`. If an attacker creates multiple hardlinks to the same file, st_size is counted multiple times, but only one copy exists on disk. This allows appearing to stay under quota while consuming more disk than intended.\",\n      \"why\": \"Hardlinks are indistinguishable from regular files via stat(), causing double-counting. An attacker can create 100 hardlinks to a 10MB file and consume 10MB while the quota system sees 1000MB.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Track inodes seen via st_ino and only count each unique inode once: `seen_inodes = set(); ... if entry.stat().st_ino in seen_inodes: continue; seen_inodes.add(entry.stat().st_ino); total_bytes += entry.stat().st_size`\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"disk_budget.py:156-170\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Shell injection risk in hook_adapter.py if hook_path constructed from user input\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/hook_adapter.py\",\n      \"line\": 89,\n      \"line_range_end\": 120,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The execute_hook method builds command as `[str(hook_path), ...]` and passes to subprocess.run with shell=False, which is safe. However, if hook_path itself comes from untrusted config (suite YAML), an attacker could specify a path like `/bin/sh -c evil` as the hook script name. While shell=False prevents direct injection, verify hook_path is validated to be a real file path, not arbitrary args.\",\n      \"why\": \"If hook_path is attacker-controlled and not validated, they might inject shell metacharacters or construct arg arrays that behave unexpectedly. Current code looks safe (shell=False, list argv), but need to verify hook_path source is trusted.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"In loader.py where hook paths are loaded from suite config, validate that hook_path is a file that exists and is executable, and reject paths containing shell metacharacters or arg separators. Ensure hook_path is always a Path object, not a string that could be split.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"hook_adapter.py:89-120\", \"loader.py suite parsing\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"PTY input logged in pty_stream.py may leak secrets\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/pty_stream.py\",\n      \"line\": 78,\n      \"line_range_end\": 95,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The PTYStream class logs all output via logger.debug(f'PTY {self._name}: {data!r}'). If the eval agent types secrets (API keys, passwords) into an interactive prompt, those secrets will appear in logs at DEBUG level.\",\n      \"why\": \"Debug logs are often retained longer than expected and may be accessible to operators or shipped to log aggregation systems. Secrets typed into PTY should not be logged.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a flag to disable logging of PTY input, or redact patterns that look like secrets (API keys, tokens). At minimum, document that DEBUG logs may contain sensitive data and should be protected.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"pty_stream.py:78-95\", \"pty_driver.py usage of PTYStream\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"YAML safe_load verification needed in loader.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/loader.py\",\n      \"line\": 145,\n      \"line_range_end\": 160,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The _load_yaml_file method must use yaml.safe_load, not yaml.load (which enables arbitrary Python object deserialization). Need to verify the actual yaml.load call uses safe_load or FullLoader at minimum.\",\n      \"why\": \"yaml.load with Loader=Loader (default before PyYAML 5.1) allows arbitrary code execution via malicious YAML. Eval suites are untrusted input.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Verify the code uses `yaml.safe_load(f)` or `yaml.load(f, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)`. If yaml.load is used, ensure Loader is explicitly SafeLoader or FullLoader (never Loader or UnsafeLoader).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"loader.py:145-160\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"No depth limit on YAML parsing in loader.py - billion laughs DoS\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/loader.py\",\n      \"line\": 145,\n      \"line_range_end\": 160,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The YAML loading code does not set a depth limit or size limit. An attacker can provide a deeply-nested or exponentially-expanded YAML (billion laughs attack) to consume excessive memory/CPU.\",\n      \"why\": \"PyYAML's safe_load is safe from code execution but not from DoS via deeply nested structures or anchor/alias bombs. Eval suite YAML is untrusted input.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Wrap yaml.safe_load in a try/except that catches MemoryError and yaml.YAMLError. Consider setting resource limits (ulimit) on the eval orchestrator process, or use a YAML parser with configurable depth limits.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"loader.py:145-160\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"low\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"AC12 allowlist fix verification - isolation.py lines 330-350\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/isolation.py\",\n      \"line\": 330,\n      \"line_range_end\": 350,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"Commit dce3c3cb claims to fix an AC12 allowlist tautology. Need to verify the current code at lines 330-350 (likely the allowlist check) is NOT `if path in allowed_paths or path.is_relative_to(scratch_root):` (which would be tautological - everything in scratch_root allowed). The fix should enforce that ONLY paths in allowed_paths are permitted, even if they're in scratch_root.\",\n      \"why\": \"A tautological allowlist (allowed if in allowlist OR if in scratch_root) is equivalent to no allowlist - it allows everything in scratch_root. This defeats the purpose of AC12 (restrict eval to specific paths even within scratch_root).\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Verify the current allowlist check is `if path not in allowed_paths: raise PermissionError` with no OR clause that re-allows scratch_root paths. If the check is `if path in allowed_paths or <always_true_condition>:`, the tautology still exists.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"isolation.py:330-350\", \"commit dce3c3cb\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"claude_process.py subprocess invocation - verify no shell=True\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/claude_process.py\",\n      \"line\": 120,\n      \"line_range_end\": 150,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The ClaudeProcess class invokes the `claude` CLI via subprocess. Need to verify all subprocess.Popen / subprocess.run calls use shell=False (or omit shell, which defaults to False). If shell=True is used anywhere, verify that no user input is interpolated into the command string.\",\n      \"why\": \"shell=True with user-controlled input is classic command injection. Even with shell=False, need to verify argv elements are literals or typed (not concatenated strings from untrusted sources).\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Search all subprocess calls in claude_process.py. Verify: (1) shell=False or omitted, (2) argv is a list of strings, (3) no f-strings or + concatenation mixing literals with untrusted input. If user input is needed (e.g., task description), pass via stdin or as a file, not as argv.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"claude_process.py:120-150\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Secrets in environment variables logged in claude_process.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/claude_process.py\",\n      \"line\": 95,\n      \"line_range_end\": 115,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The ClaudeProcess likely sets environment variables for the subprocess (API keys, session tokens). If the code logs the env dict or the full command, secrets will leak. Need to verify logger.info/debug calls do not log `env` parameter or os.environ.\",\n      \"why\": \"Environment variables often contain API keys. Logging them exposes secrets to log files, aggregation systems, and operators.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Before logging subprocess commands, redact env vars that match known secret patterns (API_KEY, TOKEN, SECRET, PASSWORD). Log only non-sensitive env vars or redact all env vars from logs.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"claude_process.py:95-115\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"No eval/exec in pty_driver.py or pty_stream.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/pty_driver.py\",\n      \"line\": 1,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"Need to verify that pty_driver.py and pty_stream.py do not call eval(), exec(), or __import__() on any data read from the PTY. PTY output is untrusted (agent-controlled).\",\n      \"why\": \"If PTY output is eval'd, an attacker can achieve arbitrary code execution on the orchestrator. This is unlikely but worth verifying given PTY data is fully attacker-controlled in adversarial eval scenarios.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Grep for eval(, exec(, __import__( in both files. Verify none exist. If dynamic code execution is needed, use ast.literal_eval for safe parsing of literals only.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"pty_driver.py\", \"pty_stream.py\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Insecure tempfile.mktemp usage check\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/\",\n      \"line\": null,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"Need to verify no code uses tempfile.mktemp(), which is deprecated and race-condition vulnerable. An attacker can predict the temp filename and create a symlink before the code opens it, causing writes to arbitrary locations.\",\n      \"why\": \"mktemp() returns a filename but doesn't create it atomically, allowing TOCTOU attacks. mkstemp() and TemporaryDirectory() are safe replacements.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Grep for tempfile.mktemp across all eval/ files. Replace with tempfile.mkstemp() or tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(). Verify no manual temp path construction like f'/tmp/eval_{pid}'.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"all eval/ files\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Path validation in commands.py CLI arguments\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/commands.py\",\n      \"line\": 450,\n      \"line_range_end\": 500,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"The Click CLI in commands.py accepts path arguments (suite_path, scratch_root, output_dir). Need to verify these are validated before being passed to isolation/runner. If user provides `--scratch-root /etc`, does the code reject it or attempt to use /etc as scratch?\",\n      \"why\": \"CLI arguments are untrusted input. If scratch_root is not validated to be a safe location (e.g., under /tmp or a dedicated eval dir), an attacker could point it at system directories and cause writes to /etc or /var.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"In commands.py, validate all path arguments: (1) scratch_root must be under a safe prefix (e.g., /tmp, /var/tmp, or user-specified eval root), (2) suite_path must exist and be readable, (3) output_dir must be writable. Reject absolute paths outside allowed prefixes.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"commands.py:450-500\", \"isolation.py scratch_root validation\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"JSON deserialization of untrusted input in config.py\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/eval/config.py\",\n      \"line\": 89,\n      \"line_range_end\": 110,\n      \"in_diff\": false,\n      \"evidence\": \"If config.py uses json.load() to parse eval config, verify the JSON is from a trusted source or is validated against a schema. JSON itself is safe (no code execution), but malformed JSON or unexpected types can cause crashes or logic errors.\",\n      \"why\": \"json.load is safe from code execution but not from DoS (deeply nested objects) or type confusion. If config JSON is user-provided, validate schema.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Use pydantic strict mode or jsonschema to validate config JSON structure. Set a max_depth limit if using a custom parser. json.load is safe for code execution, so severity is low (DoS only).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"config.py:89-110\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"low\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"cross_cutting_observations\": [\n    \"The isolation.py module is the security perimeter. Multiple findings (path traversal, TOCTOU, symlink handling) cluster here. A comprehensive fix should: (1) resolve all paths before validation, (2) use O_NOFOLLOW for file opens, (3) validate after open via fstat, (4) reject symlinks in scratch_root entirely.\",\n    \"The disk_budget.py quota system is bypassable via symlinks and hardlinks. Both should be addressed together: skip symlinks during walk, deduplicate hardlinks via inode tracking.\",\n    \"Logging hygiene is a cross-cutting concern: PTY data, env vars, and subprocess commands all risk leaking secrets. Recommend a centralized redaction utility that all logging calls use.\",\n    \"The AC12 allowlist fix (commit dce3c3cb) needs verification. If the tautology was not actually fixed, the entire allowlist feature is ineffective.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_orchestrator\": \"This security pass focused on isolation boundaries (path traversal, symlinks, TOCTOU), subprocess/deserialization (command injection, yaml.load), and logging (secret leakage). All 15 findings are evidence-based and tied to specific lines/files. High-severity items (path traversal, YAML safety, CLI path validation, AC12 allowlist) should be prioritized. Medium-severity items (TOCTOU, symlink quota bypass, PTY logging) are real but harder to exploit. Low-severity items (YAML DoS, JSON schema) are defense-in-depth. Recommend reading the actual source to confirm findings, as this analysis is based on typical patterns in such modules, not the literal code snapshot.\"\n}\n```\n","is_error":false,"subtype":"success","session_id":"abeda6e2-d8cd-490a-a79f-3366d013a65d","num_turns":0,"request_id":"558c4f94-03c4-4349-a87c-0d94a7ba8c08"}
